<https://example.org/nih-pilot/metadata/grantdocument/11123026> a pilot:GrantDocument ;
    pilot:abstractText """ABSTRACT
Africa is the cradle of many of the most important emerging zoonotic viruses of concern to the U.S., including the arboviruses chikungunya (CHIKV), Zika (ZIKV) and yellow fever (YFV), as well as the hemorrhagic fever viruses Ebola (EBOV), Marburg (MARV) and Lassa (LASV). All have a history of emergence with spread to the U.S., and some reached near pandemic proportions. Ongoing outbreaks of all underscore our continuing lack of national preparedness to anticipate, prevent and respond to emerging viruses, and our limited understanding of the mechanisms whereby zoonotic viruses emerge and spread to new hosts including humans and livestock in the U.S. and globally. Other weaknesses of our preparedness include a dearth of information on variability of disease outcomes, burdens of human exposure with risk of spread from endemic locations, diverse clinical manifestations, and the determinants of sequelae caused by genetically diverse viral variants. These critical issues can only be addressed by studying transmission and human exposure in endemic locations, which are confined to Africa. Therefore, we will capitalize on our unique network of established research sites in four countries of West Africa (Senegal, Nigeria, Liberia, and Sierra Leone) to address these critical gaps through field studies of enzootic transmission, surveillance of vectors and enzootic hosts, studies of human and livestock spillover infections, and clinical analyses and active surveillance of disease outcomes and prognostic markers. Our interdisciplinary center with broad expertise will emphasize One Health approaches along with modeling to understanding the contributions of major weather events, deforestation and other anthropogenic changes to current and future spillovers and epidemic risk. Zoonotic arboviruses will be a focus, with an emphasis on the 4 human-amplified alphaviruses and flaviviruses: CHIKV, dengue, YFV and ZIKV, along with the highly pathogenic Rift Valley fever and Crimean-Congo Hemorrhagic fever viruses. Field and laboratory research on mosquito and tick vectors will include determinants of their distribution, and impacts of weather on desiccation tolerance, microbiomes and vector competence. Surveillance will also be leveraged for virus discovery using next generation sequencing and metagenomics. In the Lassa-endemic countries of Sierra Leone, Liberia, and Nigeria, we will study and model variation in enzootic circulation among rodents according to weather patterns, geography and proximity to human settlements. Clinical surveillance will inform the natural history of acute disease and convalescence, including viral determinants of disease severity, immunity, and sequelae. We will also perform targeted ecological surveillance of bats, known reservoirs for MARV and probably EBOV, to assess and model human risk. Clinical surveillance will also trigger targeted ecologic studies of newly identified or reemerging viruses, and will be used to assess local, regional, and international risks of spread. This unique research program will directly benefit the U.S. by developing comprehensive risk models that will inform new approaches to prevent both the initiation of local African epidemics and spread of these viruses internationally.""" ;
    pilot:documentForFunding <https://example.org/nih-pilot/metadata/funding/11123026> ;
    pilot:label "West African Center for Emerging Infectious Diseases" ;
    pilot:recordedAt <https://example.org/nih-pilot/metadata/locator/stg_grant_funding.csv/2276/abstract_text>,
        <https://example.org/nih-pilot/metadata/locator/stg_grant_funding.csv/2276/appl_id>,
        <https://example.org/nih-pilot/metadata/locator/stg_grant_funding.csv/2276/award_amount>,
        <https://example.org/nih-pilot/metadata/locator/stg_grant_funding.csv/2276/award_key>,
        <https://example.org/nih-pilot/metadata/locator/stg_grant_funding.csv/2276/award_notice_date>,
        <https://example.org/nih-pilot/metadata/locator/stg_grant_funding.csv/2276/fiscal_year>,
        <https://example.org/nih-pilot/metadata/locator/stg_grant_funding.csv/2276/project_detail_url>,
        <https://example.org/nih-pilot/metadata/locator/stg_grant_funding.csv/2276/project_num>,
        <https://example.org/nih-pilot/metadata/locator/stg_grant_funding.csv/2276/project_title> ;
    pilot:sourceUrl "https://reporter.nih.gov/project-details/11123026" .

<https://example.org/nih-pilot/metadata/grantdocument/11123759> a pilot:GrantDocument ;
    pilot:abstractText """PROJECT SUMMARY
This renewal application for Center for Research in Emerging Infectious Diseases-East and Central Africa plus
(CREID-ECA+) will build upon accomplishments of Round 1 (2020-2024) where we conducted research to fill
knowledge gaps on emerging infectious diseases (EIDs) through response to 8 EID outbreaks of COVID-19,
Rift Valley fever (RVF) in Kenya and Uganda, Sudan ebolavirus (SUDV) in Uganda, Marburg in Tanzania, and
Mpox in the Democratic Republic of Congo (DRC). Direct outbreak response research (ORR) resulted in 28
manuscripts, in addition to another 53 manuscripts derived from basic research on these and other emerging
infectious viruses (EIVs) of public health importance in the East and Central Africa (ECA) region. Research on
RVFV and Middle East respiratory syndrome coronavirus (MERS-CoV) demonstrated (i) widening geographic
range and unusual transmission patterns of RVFV associated climatic variations, and (ii) low burden of camel-
to-human transmission of clade C MERS-CoV with minimal morbidity and mortality in humans. To build a
sustainable Center, we applied for 15 grants, 11 successfully; mentored 4 post-doctoral fellows; and started
training 15 PhD students, graduating five. In Round 2, we will work in both ECA and Central America regions,
continuing the overall approach of supporting outbreak response by conducting timely clinical, immunologic,
and/or socio-cultural studies that enhance disease/pathogen characterization to inform prevention and control
measures; and conducting research on maintenance, zoonotic transmission, and pathogenesis of regionally
important EIVs. In ECA, we will study MERS-CoV, orthoebolaviruses, and arboviruses (RVFV and Zika virus),
while in Central America, we will initiate studies on Zika virus and other arboviruses, hantaviruses, and
arenaviruses. One-health studies to identify ecological, climatic, and anthropogenic factors associated with the
maintenance and transmission of these viruses will utilize innovative model-guided surveillance and genomic
approaches. Continuing ORR from Round 1, we will characterize human immune response and causation of
long-term clinical sequelae following orthoebolavirus and RVFV infections using survivor cohorts in Uganda,
including comparative studies between different orthoebolavirus species and between orthoebolaviruses and
RVFV. The technical and administrative headquarters of CREID-ECA+ will be at Washington State University
(WSU) Global Health program in Nairobi, Kenya, where Kariuki Njenga (PI & Center Director) is based full-
time, while the Central America regional office will be at Universidad del Valle de Guatemala (UVG),
Guatemala City led by Celia Cordon-Rosales who will also serve as Co-PI & Deputy Center Director. Robert
Breiman (Emory Univ, Co-PI) and Eric Osoro (Univ Nairobi, Co-I) will lead our outbreak response and clinical
research activities, while Eric Lofgren (WSU) leads data stewardship and analysis team. CREID-ECA+
Centers for diagnostics and genomics will be at the International Livestock Research Institute, Kenya in ECA,
and UVG, Guatemala, in Central America, designed to minimize the need to ship samples outside the regions.""" ;
    pilot:documentForFunding <https://example.org/nih-pilot/metadata/funding/11123759> ;
    pilot:label "CENTER FOR RESEARCH IN EMERGING INFECTIOUS DISEASES- EAST AND CENTRAL AFRICA PLUS" ;
    pilot:recordedAt <https://example.org/nih-pilot/metadata/locator/stg_grant_funding.csv/2277/abstract_text>,
