<https://example.org/nih-pilot/metadata/grantdocument/11189102> a pilot:GrantDocument ;
    pilot:abstractText """IMMUNITY AND AUTOIMMUNITY INVOLVING THE NEURORETINA
For most of our uveitis studies, we utilize mouse models of autoimmune uveitis developed in our laboratory: (i) experimental autoimmune uveitis (EAU) induced by immunization with the retinal antigen (Ag) IRBP; (ii) spontaneous uveitis in R161H mice engineered to express a transgenic T cell receptor (TCR) specific for IRBP peptide 161-180.
1. Laquinimod is an aryl hydrocarbon receptor (AHR) agonist. We found that it has strong protective effects in the induced model of EAU by modulating multiple inflammatory and anti-inflammatory cytokines. Laquinimod strongly inhibited EAU in the setting of active immunization and had moderate effects in adoptive transfer settings. Data indicated that the major effect was via myeloid cells, possibly by inhibiting antigen presentation and/or effector function during inflammation. It notably inhibited immune responses to recall antigens in human PBMC, suggesting potential clinical utility (B. Xu et al, in preparation).
2. Ocular immune privilege: We have previously shown that naïve retina-specific R161H T cells, placed within the healthy living eye, recognize and respond to their antigen in situ, without inducing inflammation. After 7 days, ~1/3 converted into functional Foxp3+ T regulatory cells (Tregs). However, the cell fate of the remaining cells was unclear. Now, using single-cell transcriptomics, we find that the non-Foxp3 population adopts an anergic, rather than an effector, phenotype. RNA velocity and trajectory suggested these cells differentiated from a common precursor in parallel, rather than sequentially. This is the first molecular representation of immune privilege in the living eye, providing new insights into how the eye controls inflammation. We are also examining the transcriptome of eye-induced Tregs and how it differs from Tregs primed in other tissues or in vitro (Peng Z et al., in preparation).
3. In collaboration with the Menko lab, we further uncovered that, during autoimmune uveitis, immune cells appear to be specifically recruited to the lens capsule in the eye. They include Treg cells and M2 macrophages, and show markers associated with suppressing inflammation and resolving disease. These findings, imply a potential role for the lens in attracting cells that help to limit inflammation and promote recovery (ref #1).
4. Birdshot chorioretinopathy (BCR) is a sight-threatening inflammatory ocular disease restricted mostly to patients with the A29 HLA haplotype. Using single-cell RNA sequencing and high-dimensional flow cytometry, we analyzed immune compartments in uveitis patients. The study uncovered a unique subset of natural killer (NK) lymphocytes (CD16+ CD56dim CD8bright CD244bright), which produce proinflammatory cytokines, express cytotoxic genes, signals through the inflammatory CD244 pathway, and is strongly correlated with disease status. This subset may be involved in disease pathogenesis and could be targeted by CD244 signaling inhibitors. At minimum, it could serve as a biomarker of treatment success (ref #2)
5. In collaboration with Feng Lin's group at the Cleveland Clinic, we demonstrated that a CD6-targeted antibody-drug conjugate can be developed as a potential therapy for T cell-mediated disorders, including autoimmune uveitis (ref #3).
6. Ebola virus disease survivors experience numerous ocular sequelae. Collaborating with Verthelyi lab at the FDA, we demonstrated that all anti-Ebola glycoprotein antibodies reduced viremia, improved survival, and reduced cataracts. A particular monoclonal antibody was especially effective in reducing viral load in the eyes, inhibiting inflammation, and minimizing retinal damage (ref #4).
THE ROLE OF THE GUT MICROBIOME AND THE GUT-EYE AXIS IN UVEITIS
1. Commensal flora is necessary for developing spontaneous uveitis in the R161H model, partly due to molecular mimicry of IRBP161-180. However, innate mechanisms also play a role. Antibiotic treatment (ABX) starting a week before EAU immunization provided measurable protection, though it was temporary and disappeared with prolonged ABX. Results suggest prolonged ABX depletes intraepithelial lymphocytes (IEL), which are microbiota-dependent. We hypothesize that IEL may inhibit uveitis, an effect reversed after long-term ABX due to IEL depletion (ref #5).
2. Human gut flora and uveitis: We reconstituted germ-free R161H mice with gut microbes from three healthy human donors, establishing three humanized flora mouse lines. Results showed human gut flora supports the development of spontaneous uveitis. Mice adopted a subset of the human flora, preserving the donor's microbial footprint. Mice with high disease scores harbored more diverse gut flora than those with low scores. Verrucomicrobia, Actinobacteria, and Fusobacteria were enriched in high-score mice, while Firmicutes were enriched in low-score mice and associated with higher fecal short-chain fatty acid content. In-vivo studies are underway to examine the ability of candidate microbes to modulate autoimmune uveitis, potentially guiding interventional strategies for regulating uveitis through directed microbiome manipulation (Horai, Zhou, Murphy et al., in preparation).
3. Conversely, a collaboration with the DeFranco group at UCSF on he role of AIRE in immune tolerance to the eye, using a TCR transgenic mouse model developed in their lab, indicated that some models of spontaneous uveitis may not be dependent on gut flora (ref. # 6).
MUCOSAL IMMUNE RESPONSES TO COMMENSALS AT THE OCULAR SURFACE
Mucosal sites like the intestine, oral cavity, nasopharynx, and vagina all have associated commensal flora. The eye surface is also a mucosal site, but the presence of an ocular surface microbiome was contentious. Previously, we isolated a candidate ocular commensal, Corynebacterium mastitidis (C. mast). This organism, common to humans and mice, elicits a commensal-specific IL-17 response from T cells in the ocular mucosa, tuning local host defense to protect against pathogenic organisms.
1. We are examining the molecular sensors of C. mast in γδ (γδ) T cells. Data suggest that Vγ4 T cells' response to C. mast requires their cognate TCR. In collaboration with Benjamin Schwarz of NIAID, we are identifying the C. mast surface molecules recognized by the Vγ4 TCR. A patent application is pending based on this data, and a manuscript is in preparation (Xu, X et al.). In contrast, Vγ6 T cells respond primarily through innate receptors like TLR2 and depend heavily on IL-1. TLR2 is required not only in dendritic cells (contributing to IL-1 induction) but also in gd T cells. Intrinsic TLR2 stimulation in Vγ6 T cells upregulates the transcription factor IkBz, necessary for metabolic support of IL-17A production through fatty acid oxidation. This study highlights the importance of intrinsic TLR2 signaling in driving IL-17A production in microbiome-specific γδ T cells (X. Xu et al., BioRxiv 2024).
2. We are studying ocular surface responses to commensals in immunologically abnormal individuals, both mouse and human. In collaboration with Dr. Warren Strober's group (NIAID), who developed knock-in mice expressing a gain-of-function mutation in the NLRP3 inflammasome gene, and Dr. Raphaela Goldbach-Mansky, who treats patients with NLRP3-related diseases, we obtained evidence that an ocular surface commensal can elicit inflammation in an immunologically perturbed host, acting as a pathobiont. We are characterizing the local immune response and the ocular surface microbiome in the mouse model and patients with NLRP3 inflammasome mutations at the single-cell level (Siak, Mattapallil, St. Leger et al., in preparation). The findings will have implications for understanding and possibly for treating the characteristic ocular inflammation in patients with NLRP3-related diseases.""" ;
    pilot:documentForFunding <https://example.org/nih-pilot/metadata/funding/11189102> ;
    pilot:label "Genetic, Cellular and Molecular Mechanisms in Autoimmunity to Retina" ;
