<https://example.org/nih-pilot/metadata/grantdocument/11189391> a pilot:GrantDocument ;
    pilot:abstractText """The mission of the ‘Disease Modeling and Transmission’ (DMT) section is to study emerging and re-emerging viral pathogens that cause hemorrhagic fever, encephalitis and respiratory distress, with an aim of developing diagnostics, treatments, and vaccines. The research objectives are to learn how to interfere with the viral lifecycle and the virus-induced host responses to identify targets of intervention and to develop measures of prevention. Our research activities are targeted towards a better understanding of virus biology and virus-induced host responses to foster the development of vaccines and therapeutics.
Animal modeling plays a pivotal role in all projects and therefore pathology and pathogenesis are key components of DMT’s work. The ABSL3/4 areas of our research facility are suited for work with most rodent species, ferrets, and nonhuman primates (NHPs). In addition, we have included selected livestock species (i.e. pig, sheep and goat) and unconventional animal species such as reservoir species (i.e. rodents and bats). DMT has established breeding colony for the multimammate rat (Mastomys natalensis) to study “Old World” mammarenaviruses in their natural reservoir. Over FY23/24 we have continued to refine mouse and NHP models for Crimean-Congo hemorrhagic fever virus (CCHFV) to study pathogenesis and immune responses to the infection with the aim to ultimately develop countermeasure. We have also modeled CCHFV in livestock species, such as goats and sheep, to study transmission and intervention strategies. We have further developed the Lassa virus reservoir model in Mastomys natalensis for public health prevention measures. The Mastomys model has also been used to study Mpox infection to understand patterns of shedding and transmission. Recently we have started with animal modeling for the emerging H5N1 highly pathogenic avian influenza (HPAI) viruses that have caused outbreaks in wild birds and poultry as well as US cattle herds with rare transmission to humans. The animal health aspect is obvious, but the public health impact needs further evaluation.
DMT’s main vaccine platform is the recombinant vesicular stomatitis virus (VSV) system. The platform was developed to counteract emerging neglected viral pathogens with low worldwide infectious disease impact but of significant local/regional public health concern. The concept is emergency immunization in form of ring vaccination. DMT has continued to utilize and optimize this platform to create vaccines for many known human pathogenic emerging viral pathogens such as bunyaviruses coronaviruses, filoviruses and flaviviruses. Second-generation vectors are being developed based on the licensed VSV-EBOV vaccine vector. The concept is to utilize the favorable immune cell targeting of the Ebola glycoprotein for better foreign antigen presentation. During FY23/24 we have continued to optimize VSV-based filovirus vaccines with focus on Sudan virus. We have also revisited the aspect of postexposure prophylaxis of VSV-based vaccines for Ebola and Andes virus.
Since the COVID pandemic, DMT has included a self-amplifying RNA (repRNA) vaccine platform to its portfolio that is based on an Alphavirus RNA genome replicon and a nanostructured lipid carrier for delivery. During FY23/24 we have continued to refine the repRNA CCHFV vaccine and to understand the mechanisms and correlates of protection in the mouse and NHP models. We have successful generated repRNA vaccines for additional bunyaviruses (Severe Fever with Thrombocytopenia virus (SFTSV), Heartland virus), coronaviruses (SARS-CoV-2), enteroviruses (EV D68) and orthomyxoviruses (H5N1 HPAI). The vaccine platform is easy to manipulate and allows rapid response to emergence.
Another aspect of DMT’s portfolio are antivirals and therapeutics to treat emerging viral infections. We largely focus on repurposing of drugs/compounds that have already been in clinical testing. For efficacy testing we utilize the established animal models. During FY23/24 we have provided efficacy data for ribavirin and favipiravir in the treatment of CCHFV infections. We have tested the efficacy of monoclonal antibodies for SARS-CoV-2. We have also provided important efficacy data for the use of remdesivir in the treatment of Nipah virus infection. Currently, we are looking into antivirals for H5N1 HPAI viruses utilizing the established animal models.
Overall, during FY23/24 we have developed and refined several animal models for emerging viruses, developed and refined several vaccines and treatment options for emerging viruses. The current H5N1 outbreak in cattle in the US will be a focus for FY24/25 at least throughout the first half of that fiscal year.""" ;
    pilot:documentForFunding <https://example.org/nih-pilot/metadata/funding/11189391> ;
    pilot:label "Viral Hemorrhagic Fevers: Disease Modeling and Transmission" ;
    pilot:recordedAt <https://example.org/nih-pilot/metadata/locator/stg_grant_funding.csv/2304/abstract_text>,
