<https://example.org/nih-pilot/metadata/grantdocument/11378492> a pilot:GrantDocument ;
    pilot:abstractText """Abstract/Summary
Liberia’s history has been marred by a devastating 14-year civil war that ended in 2003. It led to emigration of scientists, researchers, and public health practitioners. The 2013-2016 Ebola Virus Disease epidemic further crippled the struggling healthcare and public health infrastructure. In response, the Fogarty International Center made it a priority to build research infrastructure and create training opportunities for Liberian scientists (PAR-16-407) to address these important issues. The Boston University and University of Liberia Emerging and Epidemic Virus Research (BULEEVR) training program in the first cycle of this grant built a partnership with key stakeholders in Liberia, including the University of Liberia, College of Health Sciences (ULCHS), Liberian Institute of Biomedical Research, National Public Health Institute of Liberia, NIH Partnership for Research on Ebola Virus in Liberia, and Research Sustainability Initiative in Liberia. In partnership with NIH PREVAIL, we have mentored 7 BULEEVR scholars – 5 of whom have matriculated into PhD programs in the United States. We created a culture of science in Liberia by organizing workshops, journal clubs, research-in-progress, and seminars amongst the different partners focused on emerging and endemic infectious diseases that are health concerns for both Liberia and the United States. This renewal builds on our successful program by continuing to train Liberian scientists and create a culture of science in Liberia. Goals for the BULEEVR are: 1) increase research competencies by training Liberian scientists in basic, translational, epidemiologic and clinical sciences with a focus on emerging and endemic pathogenic viruses; 2) provide consultation for future implementation of PhD training at ULCHS; and 3) foster collaborative research in emerging and endemic viral diseases for PhD training of Liberian researchers and BU scientists. Successful completion of these aims will build a community of Liberian and US scientists, including the next generation of teachers, research leaders and mentors, that will support a culture of rigorous, independent, and ethical science in Liberia as well as addressing infectious disease health concerns of relevance to the US. BULEEVR will also further the ability of Liberian Scientists to lead their own programs that align with US interests in addressing future public health challenges and preparedness to disease outbreaks and pandemic threats in Liberia and the United States such as current and potentially important future challenges from the Ebola virus, Lassa fever, Mpox, and HIV, thus benefitting the health of Americans. BULEEVR’s success will benefit the health of Americans by providing access to available cohorts in Liberia to study short and long term impacts of emerging infectious diseases in real time that could inform and provide insights into public responses and preparedness for future disease outbreaks in the United States as well as to efficiently test potential preventative and or therapeutic strategies that could be directly relevant to the United States.""" ;
    pilot:documentForFunding <https://example.org/nih-pilot/metadata/funding/11378492> ;
    pilot:label "Boston University and University of Liberia Emerging and Epidemic Virus Research (BULEEVR)" ;
    pilot:recordedAt <https://example.org/nih-pilot/metadata/locator/stg_grant_funding.csv/2361/abstract_text>,
        <https://example.org/nih-pilot/metadata/locator/stg_grant_funding.csv/2361/appl_id>,
