<https://example.org/nih-pilot/metadata/grantdocument/11572340> a pilot:GrantDocument ;
    pilot:abstractText """PROJECT SUMMARY
Since first discovered in 1967, members of the Filoviridae family (Ebolaviruses and Marburgviruses) have caused
>50 outbreaks in many resource-poor countries of Africa, resulting in thousands of deaths and often devastating
their public health systems and economies. Recently approved Ebola vaccines (single shot rVSV-ZEBOV and
prime/boost Ad26-ZEBOV and MVA-BN-Filo), and therapeutic monoclonal antibodies are significant additions to
our filovirus outbreak response toolkits. Though quite effective, these vaccines are reported to protect only
against the Zaire Ebola virus (EBOV), one of the many pathogenic filoviruses known to cause disease in humans.
Their efficacy to protect people against Sudan virus (SUDV), Bundibugyo virus (BDBV), Taï forest virus (TAFV)
and Marburg virus (MARV) has not been reported yet. Published data from non-human primate (NHP) models
of Ebola disease (EBOD) have shown that animals immunized with EBOV glycoprotein (GP)-based vaccines
were protected against subsequent challenge with EBOV, BDBV or TAFV, but not with SUDV or MARV,
suggesting that inclusion of GPs from EBOV, SUDV and MARV in a single vaccine will be sufficient to afford
protection against all the pathogenic filoviruses. In our preliminary studies in NHPs with bivalent Ebola virus-like
particles (VLPs) expressing GPs from EBOV and SUDV, we found that the bivalent VLPs were highly
immunogenic, elicited strong and durable anti-GP humoral and cellular immune responses that cross-reacted
with GPs from all pathogenic Ebolaviruses (EBOV, SUDV, BDBV and TAFV). In a mouse model of Ebola virus
disease (EVD), bivalent Ebola VLPs induced 100% protection against a subsequent challenge with a lethal dose
of mouse-adapted (MA)-EBOV. We have now added MARV GP to the bivalent VLPs, thus converting them into
trivalent Filo-VLPs. In our current proposal we propose to evaluate the trivalent Filo-VLPs for their pan-filovirus
vaccine potential. We hypothesize that trivalent Filo-VLPs elicit strong, broad, and durable pan-filovirus
protective immunity. We propose to test this hypothesis across different preclinical models (ferrets, hamsters,
NHPs and mice) of EBOD and Marburg disease (MARD). We will analyze early innate transcriptomic signature,
innate, and adaptive immune responses after immunization with trivalent Filo-VLPs and identify early
transcriptomic signatures that can predict late antibody and/or cellular immune responses that correlate with
protection, breadth and/or durability of anti-filovirus protective immunity. Such high value preclinical data is
absolutely needed for advancing this highly promising pan-filovirus vaccine candidate into clinical trials.""" ;
    pilot:documentForFunding <https://example.org/nih-pilot/metadata/funding/11572340> ;
    pilot:label "Evaluation of a pan-Filovirus virus-like particle (VLP) vaccine." ;
    pilot:recordedAt <https://example.org/nih-pilot/metadata/locator/stg_grant_funding.csv/2412/abstract_text>,
        <https://example.org/nih-pilot/metadata/locator/stg_grant_funding.csv/2412/appl_id>,
        <https://example.org/nih-pilot/metadata/locator/stg_grant_funding.csv/2412/award_amount>,
        <https://example.org/nih-pilot/metadata/locator/stg_grant_funding.csv/2412/award_key>,
        <https://example.org/nih-pilot/metadata/locator/stg_grant_funding.csv/2412/award_notice_date>,
        <https://example.org/nih-pilot/metadata/locator/stg_grant_funding.csv/2412/fiscal_year>,
        <https://example.org/nih-pilot/metadata/locator/stg_grant_funding.csv/2412/project_detail_url>,
        <https://example.org/nih-pilot/metadata/locator/stg_grant_funding.csv/2412/project_num>,
        <https://example.org/nih-pilot/metadata/locator/stg_grant_funding.csv/2412/project_title> ;
    pilot:sourceUrl "https://reporter.nih.gov/project-details/11572340" .

<https://example.org/nih-pilot/metadata/grantdocument/11577753> a pilot:GrantDocument ;
    pilot:abstractText """New world hemorrhagic fever arenaviruses (NWAs), such as Junín virus, are rodent-transmitted
viruses that cause ~30% mortality when they zoonose into humans. The mechanism by the NWAs
induce disease is still not certain, although it likely includes induction of high levels of cytokines
by infected sentinel cells of the immune system, leading to endothelia and thrombocyte
dysfunction and neurological disease. Survivors of Junín infection develop strong humoral
immune responses, suggesting that controlling infection at early times post-infection is critical for
virus clearance. Although an effective Junín virus vaccine has decreased disease incidence,
sporadic cases of this as well as the other known and novel NWAs for which there are no vaccines
or effective therapeutics still occur. It is well-established that the clade B pathogenic NWAs bind
to transferrin receptor 1 and other receptors on the cell surface, but the steps leading to their entry
from an acidic cellular compartment are not well-determined. We recently performed a siRNA
screen with pseudotyped viruses bearing a pathogenic Junín glycoprotein with the goal of finding
host genes involved in entry that could serve as therapeutic targets. We found that TRIM2, a
member of the tripartite motif family that includes well-known members of the host's intrinsic
defense against viral infections, limits NWA endocytosis into cells. By probing the TRIM2
interactome for other host proteins that block NWA infection, we discovered that SIRPA, a cell
surface protein that inhibits macrophage phagocytosis of tumor and dead cells and erythrocytes,
also decreases infection. Importantly, SIRPA, unlike TRIM2, inhibits infection by various human
pathogenic viruses that require trafficking to an acidic compartment, including VSV, Zika virus,
LCMV and Ebola and SARS-Cov-2 pseudoviruses. Our data suggest that TRIM2 and SIRPA act
at the viral entry/internalization step. These finding suggests that there are common mechanisms
that regulate virus endocytosis and phagocytosis.
We propose here to further investigate the overlap between virus-mediated endocytosis
and phagocytosis in vitro, ex vivo and in vivo in three aims that will 1) investigate the overlap in
the NWA entry and phagocytosis pathways; 2) determine where TRIM2/SIRPA inhibition of
infection occurs; and 3) use TRIM2, SIRPA and other relevant knockout mice to probe the roles
of these proteins in cell-type specific and in vivo infection by replication-competent NWAs. In
addition to providing mechanistic insight into the entry of NWAs into cells, these studies have the
potential of increasing our understanding as to how host factors limit infection and could lead to
new approaches to therapeutic intervention.""" ;
    pilot:documentForFunding <https://example.org/nih-pilot/metadata/funding/11577753> ;
    pilot:label "The role of TRIM2 and SIRPA in New World Arenavirus entry" ;
    pilot:recordedAt <https://example.org/nih-pilot/metadata/locator/stg_grant_funding.csv/2413/abstract_text>,
        <https://example.org/nih-pilot/metadata/locator/stg_grant_funding.csv/2413/appl_id>,
        <https://example.org/nih-pilot/metadata/locator/stg_grant_funding.csv/2413/award_amount>,
        <https://example.org/nih-pilot/metadata/locator/stg_grant_funding.csv/2413/award_key>,
        <https://example.org/nih-pilot/metadata/locator/stg_grant_funding.csv/2413/award_notice_date>,
        <https://example.org/nih-pilot/metadata/locator/stg_grant_funding.csv/2413/fiscal_year>,
        <https://example.org/nih-pilot/metadata/locator/stg_grant_funding.csv/2413/project_detail_url>,
        <https://example.org/nih-pilot/metadata/locator/stg_grant_funding.csv/2413/project_num>,
