<https://example.org/nih-pilot/metadata/grantdocument/11601594> a pilot:GrantDocument ;
    pilot:abstractText """Abstract/Summary
Liberia’s history has been marred by a devastating 14-year civil war that ended in 2003. It led to emigration of scientists, researchers, and public health practitioners. The 2013-2016 Ebola Virus Disease epidemic further crippled the struggling healthcare and public health infrastructure. In response, the Fogarty International Center made it a priority to build research infrastructure and create training opportunities for Liberian scientists (PAR-16-407) to address these important issues. The Boston University and University of Liberia Emerging and Epidemic Virus Research (BULEEVR) training program in the first cycle of this grant built a partnership with key stakeholders in Liberia, including the University of Liberia, College of Health Sciences (ULCHS), Liberian Institute of Biomedical Research, National Public Health Institute of Liberia, NIH Partnership for Research on Ebola Virus in Liberia, and Research Sustainability Initiative in Liberia. In partnership with NIH PREVAIL, we have mentored 7 BULEEVR scholars – 5 of whom have matriculated into PhD programs in the United States. We created a culture of science in Liberia by organizing workshops, journal clubs, research-in-progress, and seminars amongst the different partners focused on emerging and endemic infectious diseases that are health concerns for both Liberia and the United States. This renewal builds on our successful program by continuing to train Liberian scientists and create a culture of science in Liberia. Goals for the BULEEVR are: 1) increase research competencies by training Liberian scientists in basic, translational, epidemiologic and clinical sciences with a focus on emerging and endemic pathogenic viruses; 2) provide consultation for future implementation of PhD training at ULCHS; and 3) foster collaborative research in emerging and endemic viral diseases for PhD training of Liberian researchers and BU scientists. Successful completion of these aims will build a community of Liberian and US scientists, including the next generation of teachers, research leaders and mentors, that will support a culture of rigorous, independent, and ethical science in Liberia as well as addressing infectious disease health concerns of relevance to the US. BULEEVR will also further the ability of Liberian Scientists to lead their own programs that align with US interests in addressing future public health challenges and preparedness to disease outbreaks and pandemic threats in Liberia and the United States such as current and potentially important future challenges from the Ebola virus, Lassa fever, Mpox, and HIV, thus benefitting the health of Americans. BULEEVR’s success will benefit the health of Americans by providing access to available cohorts in Liberia to study short and long term impacts of emerging infectious diseases in real time that could inform and provide insights into public responses and preparedness for future disease outbreaks in the United States as well as to efficiently test potential preventative and or therapeutic strategies that could be directly relevant to the United States.""" ;
    pilot:documentForFunding <https://example.org/nih-pilot/metadata/funding/11601594> ;
    pilot:label "Boston University and University of Liberia Emerging and Epidemic Virus Research (BULEEVR)" ;
    pilot:recordedAt <https://example.org/nih-pilot/metadata/locator/stg_grant_funding.csv/2416/abstract_text>,
        <https://example.org/nih-pilot/metadata/locator/stg_grant_funding.csv/2416/appl_id>,
        <https://example.org/nih-pilot/metadata/locator/stg_grant_funding.csv/2416/award_amount>,
        <https://example.org/nih-pilot/metadata/locator/stg_grant_funding.csv/2416/award_key>,
        <https://example.org/nih-pilot/metadata/locator/stg_grant_funding.csv/2416/award_notice_date>,
        <https://example.org/nih-pilot/metadata/locator/stg_grant_funding.csv/2416/fiscal_year>,
        <https://example.org/nih-pilot/metadata/locator/stg_grant_funding.csv/2416/project_detail_url>,
        <https://example.org/nih-pilot/metadata/locator/stg_grant_funding.csv/2416/project_num>,
        <https://example.org/nih-pilot/metadata/locator/stg_grant_funding.csv/2416/project_title> ;
    pilot:sourceUrl "https://reporter.nih.gov/project-details/11601594" .

<https://example.org/nih-pilot/metadata/grantdocument/11603087> a pilot:GrantDocument ;
    pilot:abstractText """The Democratic Republic of the Congo (DRC) is a global hotspot for emerging and re-emerging infectious
diseases, including Ebola virus disease, mpox, and multi-drug-resistant tuberculosis (MDR-TB). These
pathogens pose ongoing threats to global health security, including the United States, due to increasing travel
and interconnected health systems. The DRC offers a unique setting for research and training, with access to
outbreaks, diverse pathogen ecology, and clinical populations not readily available in the United States, enabling
the generation of knowledge directly relevant to protecting American health. We propose the Emerging and Re-
Emerging Pathogens Research Training Program in the DRC (EREP-RTP-DRC), based at the National Institute
of Biomedical Research (INRB), in partnership with Boston University, the University of California, Los Angeles,
and the University of Pittsburgh.
The overall objective is to develop a critical mass of highly skilled scientists and technical professionals capable
of conducting rigorous research and responding to emerging infectious disease threats, particularly highconsequence
pathogens of direct relevance to U.S. national health security such as Ebola virus disease, mpox,
and MDR-TB. This directly supports U.S. and global health priorities by strengthening surveillance, early
detection, and response systems for pathogens with pandemic potential. The specific aims are to: 1) provide
research training through long-term degree programs and short-term training opportunities; 2) support mentored
research on emerging and re-emerging infectious diseases with pandemic potential; 3) strengthen competencies
in scientific communication, grantsmanship, research ethics, and program management; and 4) evaluate
program outcomes and enhance the translation of research findings into clinical and public health practice,
including relevance to the United States.
We will achieve these aims using an innovative North–South and South–South triangular mentoring model. Each
trainee will have one primary mentor based in the DRC and at least one co-mentor from collaborating institutions
in the United States. Most long-term training and research will be conducted in the DRC, while short-term training
may take place at U.S. partner institutions. After consultation with an independent Training Advisory Committee,
the Steering Committee will oversee the recruitment, research projects, monitoring, and evaluation of 19 highly
competitive trainees (7 Master’s, 8 PhD, 3 postdoctoral fellows, and 1 laboratory technician), each supported
with a seed research grant and protected research time. Each trainee will have an individualized career
development plan monitored during training and for five years following program completion.
By strengthening research capacity at the source of high-risk pathogens (e.g., Ebola virus, mpox virus, MDRTB),
this program will generate knowledge, tools, and a trained workforce that directly contribute to protecting
the health of Americans and improving preparedness for future infectious disease threats.""" ;
    pilot:documentForFunding <https://example.org/nih-pilot/metadata/funding/11603087> ;
    pilot:label "Emerging and Re-Emerging Pathogens Research Training Program in DRC (EREP-RTP-DRC)" ;
