emorrhagic fever viruses. Field and laboratory research on mosquito and tick vectors will include determinants of their distribution, and impacts of weather on desiccation tolerance, microbiomes and vector competence. Surveillance will also be leveraged for virus discovery using next generation sequencing and metagenomics. In the Lassa-endemic countries of Sierra Leone, Liberia, and Nigeria, we will study and model variation in enzootic circulation among rodents according to weather patterns, geography and proximity to human settlements. Clinical surveillance will inform the natural history of acute disease and convalescence, including viral determinants of disease severity, immunity, and sequelae. We will also perform targeted ecological surveillance of bats, known reservoirs for MARV and probably EBOV, to assess and model human risk. Clinical surveillance will also trigger targeted ecologic studies of newly identified or reemerging viruses, and will be used to assess local, regional, and international risks of spread. This unique research program will directly benefit the U.S. by developing comprehensive risk models that will inform new approaches to prevent both the initiation of local African epidemics and spread of these viruses internationally.""" ;
