<https://example.org/nih-pilot/metadata/grantdocument/11616349> a pilot:GrantDocument ;
    pilot:abstractText """ABSTRACT
Africa is the cradle of many of the most important emerging zoonotic viruses of concern to the U.S., including the arboviruses chikungunya (CHIKV), Zika (ZIKV) and yellow fever (YFV), as well as the hemorrhagic fever viruses Ebola (EBOV), Marburg (MARV) and Lassa (LASV). All have a history of emergence with spread to the U.S., and some reached near pandemic proportions. Ongoing outbreaks of all underscore our continuing lack of national preparedness to anticipate, prevent and respond to emerging viruses, and our limited understanding of the mechanisms whereby zoonotic viruses emerge and spread to new hosts including humans and livestock in the U.S. and globally. Other weaknesses of our preparedness include a dearth of information on variability of disease outcomes, burdens of human exposure with risk of spread from endemic locations, diverse clinical manifestations, and the determinants of sequelae caused by genetically diverse viral variants. These critical issues can only be addressed by studying transmission and human exposure in endemic locations, which are confined to Africa. Therefore, we will capitalize on our unique network of established research sites in four countries of West Africa (Senegal, Nigeria, Liberia, and Sierra Leone) to address these critical gaps through field studies of enzootic transmission, surveillance of vectors and enzootic hosts, studies of human and livestock spillover infections, and clinical analyses and active surveillance of disease outcomes and prognostic markers. Our interdisciplinary center with broad expertise will emphasize One Health approaches along with modeling to understanding the contributions of major weather events, deforestation and other anthropogenic changes to current and future spillovers and epidemic risk. Zoonotic arboviruses will be a focus, with an emphasis on the 4 human-amplified alphaviruses and flaviviruses: CHIKV, dengue, YFV and ZIKV, along with the highly pathogenic Rift Valley fever and Crimean-Congo Hemorrhagic fever viruses. Field and laboratory research on mosquito and tick vectors will include determinants of their distribution, and impacts of weather on desiccation tolerance, microbiomes and vector competence. Surveillance will also be leveraged for virus discovery using next generation sequencing and metagenomics. In the Lassa-endemic countries of Sierra Leone, Liberia, and Nigeria, we will study and model variation in enzootic circulation among rodents according to weather patterns, geography and proximity to human settlements. Clinical surveillance will inform the natural history of acute disease and convalescence, including viral determinants of disease severity, immunity, and sequelae. We will also perform targeted ecological surveillance of bats, known reservoirs for MARV and probably EBOV, to assess and model human risk. Clinical surveillance will also trigger targeted ecologic studies of newly identified or reemerging viruses, and will be used to assess local, regional, and international risks of spread. This unique research program will directly benefit the U.S. by developing comprehensive risk models that will inform new approaches to prevent both the initiation of local African epidemics and spread of these viruses internationally.""" ;
    pilot:documentForFunding <https://example.org/nih-pilot/metadata/funding/11616349> ;
    pilot:label "West African Center for Emerging Infectious Diseases" ;
    pilot:recordedAt <https://example.org/nih-pilot/metadata/locator/stg_grant_funding.csv/2425/abstract_text>,
        <https://example.org/nih-pilot/metadata/locator/stg_grant_funding.csv/2425/appl_id>,
